{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cej3Wm5EFhNaY5AUE6axS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019753QvH52fp1nQHsjqYX7q","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/diytechy/.claude/projects/-Users-diytechy-Documents-ai-template/memory/adjudication-reread-loop-byte-identical-rows.md","old_string":"Related: [[loop-review-runaway-2026-09-03]],\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]].","new_string":"**The counterexample, 2026-09-04 (WI-590, same four rows).** The check above is\nwhat tells the two cases apart, and it must be run before inheriting anything.\nHere the registry log was NOT empty: WI-587 (`64692ddf`) and WI-588\n(`95686911`, `6f274193`) had landed the repairs, and every one of WI-586's\nfindings re-drove as DISCHARGED. Verdict was a MIXED batch — LLR-208/TC-206\napproved, LLR-207/TC-205 returned on a finding that did not exist at the prior\nround.\n\nTwo things that round taught:\n\n- **A merged fix can import a new defect into an untouched row.** `f4ca1bd5`\n  gave `governing_rev` a second disposable-commit class (the mechanical close)\n  *after* `LLR-207.detail` was last written, so the row went stale without\n  anyone editing it — and its `code_symbol`, its TC, and every requirements\n  registry were silent on a public `__all__` export. On a repeat adjudication,\n  diff the row's module against the row's own last-edit commit; the interesting\n  defect is usually younger than the text. See\n  [[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].\n- **Mutation-test the fix, not just the finding.** A `sed`/regex deletion that\n  breaks syntax reds the whole module and proves nothing — `ast.parse` the\n  mutant and confirm the symbol count dropped to 0 before believing a red. The\n  valid arm here took `tests/test_trunk_step.py` from `17 passed` to exactly\n  `1 failed, 16 passed`, naming the step that stopped running.\n\nRelated: [[loop-review-runaway-2026-09-03]],\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]],\n[[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]]."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1144,"cache_read_input_tokens":144326,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1144},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50323fe1-9775-40d3-be96-f82d47db04c4","uuid":"7f322413-051b-4909-b5da-0979d7804ac9","request_id":"req_011Cej3WkCtQWpBpPDaPwQqq"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019753QvH52fp1nQHsjqYX7q","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/diytechy/.claude/projects/-Users-diytechy-Documents-ai-template/memory/adjudication-reread-loop-byte-identical-rows.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50323fe1-9775-40d3-be96-f82d47db04c4","uuid":"d5883ea2-03fc-4860-a105-e680d3dab2da","timestamp":"2026-09-04T20:39:06.514Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/.claude/projects/-Users-diytechy-Documents-ai-template/memory/adjudication-reread-loop-byte-identical-rows.md","oldString":"Related: [[loop-review-runaway-2026-09-03]],\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]].","newString":"**The counterexample, 2026-09-04 (WI-590, same four rows).** The check above is\nwhat tells the two cases apart, and it must be run before inheriting anything.\nHere the registry log was NOT empty: WI-587 (`64692ddf`) and WI-588\n(`95686911`, `6f274193`) had landed the repairs, and every one of WI-586's\nfindings re-drove as DISCHARGED. Verdict was a MIXED batch — LLR-208/TC-206\napproved, LLR-207/TC-205 returned on a finding that did not exist at the prior\nround.\n\nTwo things that round taught:\n\n- **A merged fix can import a new defect into an untouched row.** `f4ca1bd5`\n  gave `governing_rev` a second disposable-commit class (the mechanical close)\n  *after* `LLR-207.detail` was last written, so the row went stale without\n  anyone editing it — and its `code_symbol`, its TC, and every requirements\n  registry were silent on a public `__all__` export. On a repeat adjudication,\n  diff the row's module against the row's own last-edit commit; the interesting\n  defect is usually younger than the text. See\n  [[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].\n- **Mutation-test the fix, not just the finding.** A `sed`/regex deletion that\n  breaks syntax reds the whole module and proves nothing — `ast.parse` the\n  mutant and confirm the symbol count dropped to 0 before believing a red. The\n  valid arm here took `tests/test_trunk_step.py` from `17 passed` to exactly\n  `1 failed, 16 passed`, naming the step that stopped running.\n\nRelated: [[loop-review-runaway-2026-09-03]],\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]],\n[[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].","originalFile":"---\nname: adjudication-reread-loop-byte-identical-rows\ndescription: An adjudication lane can re-judge the SAME row text many times because it may flip a Status but never edit the cells its findings name — check for registry commits on the lane before trusting or re-deriving a prior verdict\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 3aa25326-d0fa-4e17-bf43-3979ded3d7c7\n---\n\nWI-586 (LLR-207/LLR-208/TC-205/TC-206) was adjudicated three times on\n2026-09-03 against **byte-identical** row text. The lane's own commits touched\nonly `docs/iteration/`, `docs/reviews/` and its spec — never the registries.\n\n**Why:** an adjudicator's authority is the `Status` flip plus the anchoring\nsnapshot. It may NOT edit the `Detail`/`Method`/`Evidence` cells its findings\nname — that repair travels only through the `## Dispositions` drafts, which\nintake mints at the row's merge. So a RETURN verdict cannot make the next\nreread come out differently. The rows can only change after the merge.\n\n**How to apply:**\n\n- FIRST establish whether the text moved:\n  `git log --oneline <lane-base>..HEAD -- docs/requirements/... docs/test/...`.\n  Empty means the rows are exactly what the prior verdict judged.\n- That fact cuts both ways. It does NOT license inheriting the earlier\n  findings — re-drive them (mutation-test any \"no test covers X\" claim; a\n  guard whose deletion leaves the suite green is a confirmed gap). It DOES\n  mean the useful next act is the merge that mints the drafts, not a further\n  reread; say so in the verdict as an observation for the coordinator.\n- Check for an outstanding CHANGES-REQUESTED against the *dispositions* and\n  apply it — that rework is the session's real deliverable on a repeat round,\n  and it is what unblocks the mint. Verify with\n  `intake.parse_dispositions(text, path)` → `refusal=None` and the expected\n  draft count.\n- `bar` on a disposition draft must match the deliverable: new pytest\n  regressions are `DevStg-Tests` (14 steps), not `DevStg-Reqs` (12) — the\n  extra two are `design-flows` and `trajectory` (`kitlib/ladder.py:79`).\n- pytest lives in the TRUNK venv, not the lane:\n  `/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template/.venv/bin/python`. The commit hook\n  runs `/usr/local/bin/python3`, so the `format`/ruff step SKIPs loudly —\n  environment, not a regression (see [[ruff-0-16-skew-scaffold-lint-failures]]).\n\nRelated: [[loop-review-runaway-2026-09-03]],\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]].\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":40,"oldLines":5,"newStart":40,"newLines":30,"lines":["   runs `/usr/local/bin/python3`, so the `format`/ruff step SKIPs loudly —","   environment, not a regression (see [[ruff-0-16-skew-scaffold-lint-failures]])."," ","+**The counterexample, 2026-09-04 (WI-590, same four rows).** The check above is","+what tells the two cases apart, and it must be run before inheriting anything.","+Here the registry log was NOT empty: WI-587 (`64692ddf`) and WI-588","+(`95686911`, `6f274193`) had landed the repairs, and every one of WI-586's","+findings re-drove as DISCHARGED. Verdict was a MIXED batch — LLR-208/TC-206","+approved, LLR-207/TC-205 returned on a finding that did not exist at the prior","+round.","+","+Two things that round taught:","+","+- **A merged fix can import a new defect into an untouched row.** `f4ca1bd5`","+  gave `governing_rev` a second disposable-commit class (the mechanical close)","+  *after* `LLR-207.detail` was last written, so the row went stale without","+  anyone editing it — and its `code_symbol`, its TC, and every requirements","+  registry were silent on a public `__all__` export. On a repeat adjudication,","+  diff the row's module against the row's own last-edit commit; the interesting","+  defect is usually younger than the text. See","+  [[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].","+- **Mutation-test the fix, not just the finding.** A `sed`/regex deletion that","+  breaks syntax reds the whole module and proves nothing — `ast.parse` the","+  mutant and confirm the symbol count dropped to 0 before believing a red. The","+  valid arm here took `tests/test_trunk_step.py` from `17 passed` to exactly","+  `1 failed, 16 passed`, naming the step that stopped running.","+"," Related: [[loop-review-runaway-2026-09-03]],","-[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]].","+[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]],","+[[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]]."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cej3Xq1e2xN5skz4z9xoe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. **Mixed batch — `OUTCOME: RETURN rows=4`.**\n\n## What I ruled\n\n**APPROVED (flipped + anchored):** LLR-208, TC-206 — the SR-170 rollup chain.\n**RETURNED (left `Drafted`):** LLR-207, TC-205 — the SR-156 verdict-record chain.\n\n## Why this wasn't another reread\n\nThese four rows had been adjudicated six times under WI-586 against byte-identical text. First thing I checked was whether the text had actually moved — it had: WI-587 and WI-588 landed the repairs. So I re-drove every prior finding rather than inheriting it, and **all of WI-586's findings are discharged** (the HEAD-vs-branch-tip clause, the peel-terminus clause, all 46 TC-205 citations resolving to passing tests, the CMP-006 note, the trunk-wiring gap).\n\n## The approvals\n\nLLR-208's six detail claims all verify TRUE against `gen_verdict_rollup.py`. The load-bearing one — that the trunk step actually regenerates the rollup — was WI-586's sole ground for returning the pair, so I mutation-tested it myself rather than trusting the report. My first attempt was a regex that broke the module's syntax and proved nothing; the corrected arm (`ast.parse` clean, `verdict-rollup` occurrences → 0) took `tests/test_trunk_step.py` from `17 passed` to exactly `1 failed, 16 passed`, failing on a message that *names the step that stopped running*. Restored → `17 passed`. That gap is genuinely closed.\n\n## The return — a new defect, younger than the row text\n\n`LLR-207.detail` says `governing_rev` peels \"any verified refresh it meets.\" It doesn't. It peels through `_peel_target`, whose own docstring reads **\"TWO commits are disposable and this is their one home\"** — the station refresh *and* `mechanical_close_attestation`, the machinery's own adjudication close.\n\nThat second class is a public `__all__` export that moves the governing identity on real lanes, and it is described by **no row in any requirements registry** — zero grep hits. So LLR-207 isn't one of several possible homes; it's the only one. A builder implementing this cell literally builds a one-class peel and reproduces exactly the defect `f4ca1bd5` repaired.\n\nTC-205 returns with it: three tests for that class exist and pass, and are cited by **no test case anywhere**. The coverage exists; the record is silent about it.\n\nThe cause is worth naming: `f4ca1bd5` merged *after* LLR-207's last edit. The row went stale without anyone touching it — a merged fix importing a defect into an untouched row.\n\n## Two things for your attention\n\n- The `format` check **SKIPped on both commits** (ruff not importable by `/usr/local/bin/python3`). Environment skew, not a regression, and both commits are docs-only — but it is a declared bar that did not grade them.\n- TC-205 declares `Tier = \"Smoke\"` while 8 of its 46 citations sit in `SLOW_MODULES` and never run in smoke. I recorded it as a secondary, rulable-either-way item rather than a blocking finding, since it's a known unenforced Tier/marker non-join and sibling TC-132 cites the same module at `Full`.\n\nThe returned half can't be fixed by another reread — an adjudicator may flip a Status, never edit the cells its findings name. The useful next act is this row's merge, which mints the disposition (verified through `intake.parse_dispositions`: `refusal=None`, 1 draft, `bar='DevStg-Tests'`)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1010,"cache_read_input_tokens":145470,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1010},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50323fe1-9775-40d3-be96-f82d47db04c4","uuid":"cebad336-8204-48a2-9992-76f3f6b39749","request_id":"req_011Cej3XovA5bMCAp2PR9hBV"}
